## Drift and ignored changes

Outside pending draft recovery, changing only `timeouts` leaves the remote Content Type untouched. Managed updates write the complete draft, including values retained by `ignore_changes`. An external draft whose changes are all ignored is left untouched. Deactivating outside Terraform is reflected in state; Terraform does not reactivate until a later managed change writes a draft eligible for activation.

Imported fields remain readable even when their definitions would fail validation on an update.

## Remove a field

Remove a field from an activated Content Type in two applies:

1. Set `omitted = true` on the field and apply. Terraform activates the draft with the field omitted from delivery responses.
2. Remove the field from `fields` and apply again.

~> The second apply permanently deletes the field. Check that your applications work with the field omitted before proceeding. See Contentful's [field deletion workflow](https://www.contentful.com/developers/docs/references/content-management-api/content-types/#deleting-fields).

## Destroy

Destroy deactivates the Content Type before deleting it. If deactivation fails for a reason other than an already absent or unpublished Content Type, deletion stops. These requests do not send a version or ETag precondition, so changes made outside Terraform since the last refresh do not prevent deletion.

## Activation recovery

If activation fails after Contentful accepts a draft, inspect the Content Type and run `terraform plan`. Creation can finish with a warning. A `VersionMismatch` ends automatic recovery immediately, including on the first activation attempt. An unchanged apply then leaves the draft alone.

For other activation request failures, Terraform can retry the recorded draft version without writing it again. Refresh checks that the same draft still needs activation. If the original draft write was never confirmed, Terraform has no version it can safely activate. See [recovery after a failed write](../guides/operation-timeouts#recover-after-a-failed-write).

